; Scry core grammar -- Phase 1 subset.
;
; This file is the hand-written SOURCE for a generated file:
; `lib/scry_core/grammar/compiled.ex` (`Scry.Core.Grammar.Compiled`,
; what `Scry.Core.parse/1` actually runs queries through) is produced
; from this file by `priv/gen/generate_compiled_grammar.exs` -- after
; editing anything below, run `mix run priv/gen/generate_compiled_grammar.exs`
; and re-run the test suite; never hand-edit the generated file itself.
;
; Covers a deliberately bounded slice of lang_spec.md so the core/variant
; composition architecture (impl_spec.md §2/§4) can be proven against a
; real parser before the full language is written out. Phase 1 has:
;
;   - `#` line comments and `;` block comments (lang_spec §3) -- the
;     latter genuinely is a context-free, not regular, construct
;     (consuming its own matching `}` via `{`/`}` depth counting), so it
;     cannot be a plain lexer-level token the way `#`/SPACE are; it
;     needs Aether's own `@native`/`CustomLexeme` escape hatch instead
;     (`BLOCK_COMMENT`'s own comment below, `Scry.Core.Grammar.
;     BlockCommentLexeme`'s moduledoc has the full mechanics)
;   - bare identifiers + dot-path, backtick-escaped on keyword collision
;     (`` `select` ``, §3)
;   - double- and single-quoted strings with real escape handling
;     (`\" \' \\ \n \t \uXXXX`, §4), triple-quoted multiline strings
;     (verbatim content, no indentation stripping -- lang_spec §4 says
;     nothing more specific, see MULTILINE_STRING's own comment for the
;     reasoning), integers, decimals, `2/3`-shaped rational literals,
;     radix integers (`0x`/`0o`/`0b`), list literals (`[...]`, usable
;     anywhere a literal is, not just inside `in [...]`), atom literals
;     (`:name`, tagged `{:atom, name}` rather than a real Elixir atom --
;     Scry.Core.Actions' own handle_token(:ATOM, ...) explains why), ISO
;     8601 date/timestamp literals (a real `Date`/`NaiveDateTime`/
;     `DateTime`, per what's present -- calendar validation happens via
;     the stdlib in Scry.Core.Actions, not in the grammar), regex sigils
;     (`@r/.../ `, any single-byte delimiter -- `SIGIL`'s own comment has
;     the mechanics, via Aether's `@native`/`CustomLexeme` escape hatch)
;     paired with the `~` match operator,
;     nil/true/false (§4), duration/byte-size literals (`DURATION`/
;     `BYTE_SIZE`'s own comments have the concrete unit vocabulary
;     chosen, mirrored in lang_spec.md itself) -- the rest of the
;     exact-rational tower's own remaining gap is `inexact`/float
;     coexistence specifically (see Scry.Core.Rational's own moduledoc);
;     arithmetic *expressions* are implemented (§5.10, `expression`'s
;     own comment below)
;   - `select`/`where`, `and`/`or`/`not`, comparisons, `in [...]`, and the
;     full §5.2 header-modifier chain (`group by`/`having`/`distinct`/
;     `order by`/`limit ... offset`, in their required fixed order) (§5)
;     -- `group by ... rollup`/`... cube` and the "having requires an
;     aggregate expression in the body" *compile-time* check (it's
;     enforced at *execution* time now -- Scry.Core.Executor's own
;     `aggregate_query?/1` -- but nothing statically validates it during
;     parsing) still deferred. §5's own aggregates/casts/window functions
;     (`sum`/`avg`/`count`/`min`/`max`/`stddev_*`/`var_*`/`percentile`,
;     `string`/`int`/`exact`/`inexact`/`json`, `over`/`row_number`/`rank`/
;     `first_value`/`last_value`), §6's correlation/computed
;     fields/`WHEN`, and §9's `WITH`/`FRAGMENT` are all implemented (see
;     each construct's own comment below for the real, current state --
;     this list goes stale fast and isn't re-verified against it). §7's
;     `TYPE` declaration *shape* parses (`type_decl`'s own comment below
;     has the "parsed, not yet consumed" scope) -- the full type system
;     (union-type checking, flow-sensitive null narrowing, a schema
;     registry) does not. §8's five standard variants (graph/time-series/
;     document/logic/search) remain entirely unimplemented -- no kind
;     fragment exists in this codebase yet to compose in.
;   - one EP1(a) extension point (`select_ep1a`, lang_spec §2), proving
;     the union-of-simultaneous-kind-contributions composition pattern
;     the whole design actually needs (not "core references a rule it
;     never defines" -- core supplies a real always-fails default, see
;     `select_ep1a`'s own comment below for why that turned out to
;     matter)
;
; Every deferral above is a real gap, not a design decision -- each one
; needs its own pass. See impl_spec.md §8 for how this file's own
; composition gets tested (Scry.Core.GrammarCompose + its test suite).

@grammar "scry_core"
@root document
@case_insensitive
@skip TRIVIA

; ---- trivia ----

COMMENT := "#" (!"\n" .)*
TRIVIA  := (SPACE | COMMENT)*

; `;` block comments (lang_spec §3) -- Scry.Core.Grammar.BlockCommentLexeme's
; own moduledoc has the full mechanics (Aether's `@native`/`CustomLexeme`
; escape hatch, verified via scratch grammar first, the same way `SIGIL`'s
; own "any delimiter" support was). Genuinely a context-free construct
; (nested `{`/`}` depth counting) no plain token pattern could express.
;
; Deliberately *not* folded into `TRIVIA` -- confirmed empirically (the
; hard way, a real `Grammar.VM.Linker` crash caught before this reached
; `develop`) that a `CustomLexeme` token can never be referenced from
; inside *another* token's own body (`guides/aether/AETHER.md`'s own
; `@native` section says as much -- "can't be referenced from inside
; another token's body, and can't be composed inside a larger token
; expression" -- a constraint this file's own `SIGIL` change didn't run
; into, since `SIGIL` is only ever referenced from a *rule* (`literal`),
; never from inside `TRIVIA` itself). Wired in at the *rule* level
; instead, exactly where lang_spec's own "valid only immediately before
; a block-opening keyword" wording already points -- see `document`'s
; own comment for the `*_or_comment` wrapper-rule shape this uses,
; verified against `Grammar.VM` specifically (the interpreted backend
; this project's own test suite runs against, not just the native
; codegen backend a first pass verified against and wrongly trusted was
; equivalent).
BLOCK_COMMENT := @native("Scry.Core.Grammar.BlockCommentLexeme", "scan")

; ---- identifiers / keywords ----
;
; IDENT feeds keyword reclassification below -- every structural keyword
; this phase needs is a *reclassified* IDENT match, not its own token,
; so declaration order between them and IDENT never matters (only the
; reclassification does).
;
; @refine, not @keywords: @keywords' table lookup is an exact,
; case-sensitive match against the token's raw text, and does not pick
; up @case_insensitive -- confirmed empirically, not a guess (see
; Scry.Core.Grammar.KeywordRefiner's moduledoc). @refine hands the whole
; decision to that module instead, which downcases first.
;
; No `-` in the trailing character class (nor in ESCAPED_IDENT/ATOM/
; PARAM below, which originally copied this same class) -- lang_spec
; §3's own "Identifiers" section says only "Bare by default (`name`,
; `orders.total`)", never mentions hyphens as valid identifier
; characters at all. Allowing them was an unjustified overreach from
; this file's own first draft, found while scoping arithmetic
; operators: with `-` in IDENT's own class, maximal munch would read
; `price-quantity` as one identifier, never as `price` `MINUS`
; `quantity` -- silently making subtraction impossible to write without
; spaces around it. Fixed at the source instead of routed around.
IDENT := [[:alpha:]_][[:alnum:]_]*
  @refine("Scry.Core.Grammar.KeywordRefiner", "refine",
          KW_SELECT, KW_WHERE, KW_AND, KW_OR, KW_NOT, KW_IN,
          KW_NIL, KW_TRUE, KW_FALSE,
          KW_GROUP, KW_BY, KW_HAVING, KW_DISTINCT, KW_ORDER,
          KW_LIMIT, KW_OFFSET, KW_DESC, KW_ASC, KW_REQUIRED, KW_IF,
          KW_WHEN, KW_THEN, KW_ELSE, KW_FRAGMENT, KW_WITH,
          KW_UNION, KW_ALL, KW_INTERSECT, KW_EXCEPT,
          KW_OVER, KW_PARTITION, KW_ROWS, KW_BETWEEN, KW_PRECEDING,
          KW_FOLLOWING, KW_CURRENT, KW_ROW, KW_UNBOUNDED, KW_TYPE,
          KW_ROLLUP, KW_CUBE)

; A field name that happens to collide with a keyword (lang_spec §3:
; "Escaped with backtick only on keyword collision") -- a wholly
; separate token from IDENT, not something IDENT itself can produce no
; matter what it matches, so it's never subject to `@refine` above:
; backtick-escaped text is *always* a plain identifier, regardless of
; whether the text between the backticks happens to also be spelled
; like a keyword. `select`/`` `SELECT` ``/`` `Select` `` (any case, per
; lang_spec §3's own "collision check is case-insensitive too") are all
; the identifier "select" this way, never KW_SELECT.
ESCAPED_IDENT := "`" [[:alpha:]_][[:alnum:]_]* "`"

; Colon-*prefixed* (lang_spec §5.3: "`:active` is colon-prefixed;
; `subtotal:` is colon-suffixed" -- the latter, an alias/named
; body-item marker, is a separate, still-deferred construct, and
; doesn't collide with this). Never subject to `@refine` -- a
; completely separate token from IDENT, same reasoning as
; ESCAPED_IDENT immediately above.
ATOM := ":" [[:alpha:]_][[:alnum:]_]*

; External parameter (lang_spec §5.7/§9): a value supplied out-of-band
; at submission time via a separate variables payload, never
; string-interpolated into query text -- a real injection-safety
; property, not style. `$` doesn't collide with anything else declared
; in this file. Parsing only ever produces the placeholder
; `{:param, name}` (Scry.Core.Actions' own handle_token(:PARAM, ...));
; resolving it against a real caller-supplied value happens entirely
; at execution time, in Scry.Core.Executor -- see its own moduledoc.
PARAM := "$" [[:alpha:]_][[:alnum:]_]*

; ---- literals ----
;
; Three mutually-exclusive number tokens, disambiguated by maximal munch
; alone (Aether's own lexer rule: longest match at a given position wins
; -- see guides/aether/AETHER.md's "Maximal munch") -- declaration order
; between them is irrelevant, since a given input position can only ever
; actually match one of the three shapes to begin with:
;
;   - DECIMAL requires a literal "." (`DIGIT+ "." DIGIT+`), so it simply
;     doesn't match plain digits at all (`30` never reaches DECIMAL,
;     only INTEGER)
;   - RADIX requires the literal "0" + a base letter, strictly longer
;     than the single "0" digit INTEGER alone could match at that same
;     position (`0x1F`: RADIX consumes all 4 characters, INTEGER only 1)
;
; `[xX]`/`[oO]`/`[bB]` spell out both cases explicitly rather than
; relying on `@case_insensitive` -- that pragma only affects bare
; *string* literals (`"lit"`), never character classes, per the same
; guide's own `@case_insensitive` section.
INTEGER := DIGIT+
DECIMAL := DIGIT+ "." DIGIT+
RADIX   := "0" [xX] HEX+
         | "0" [oO] [0-7]+
         | "0" [bB] [01]+

; Duration/byte-size literals (lang_spec §4 -- the table gives worked
; examples, `1h`/`5m`/`10MB`/`10MiB`, not a full unit enumeration; this
; is the concrete vocabulary chosen to fill that gap, mirrored in
; lang_spec.md itself so the two stay in sync). Both collapse into the
; ordinary exact-rational tower exactly like `RADIX` already does
; (`Scry.Core.Actions`' own `handle_token` multiplies the magnitude by
; the unit's own canonical value via `Scry.Core.Rational.mul/2`) -- a
; canonical *base* unit each (seconds for duration, bytes for byte
; size), not a distinct wrapper type; `500ms` is simply the exact value
; `1/2`, indistinguishable from writing that fraction directly, the
; same "enters the tower as a plain value" precedent `RADIX`'s own
; comment above already established.
;
; `"unit"cs` on every alternative -- forces case-sensitivity for this
; one literal, overriding this grammar's own file-wide
; `@case_insensitive` pragma (guides/aether/AETHER.md's own "String
; literals" section: "a quote suffix controls case sensitivity for that
; one literal ... `cs` -- always case-sensitive, regardless of the
; grammar-level setting"). Deliberate, not an oversight: every real
; system using this exact suffix vocabulary (Prometheus, Kubernetes,
; Go's own `time.ParseDuration`, AWS) treats it case-sensitively --
; `5M` (bare, no `B`) or `5MS` (wrong case) are neither a valid duration
; nor a valid byte size, confirmed empirically (scratch grammar) to
; fail cleanly rather than silently matching the wrong unit.
;
; Maximal munch resolves everything else the same way `RADIX` already
; documents above: `10MiB` needs its own listed 3-character alternative
; to win over the 2-character `MB`/`KB`/etc. ones (both are real,
; distinct alternatives here, not a prefix relationship needing special
; care), and `500ms` needs `ms` to win over the 1-character `s` --
; confirmed empirically, not assumed, before relying on either.
;
; No `w` (week) -- deliberately excluded, not an oversight: unlike every
; other unit here, "a week" is not an unambiguous fixed duration without
; a calendar (7 exact days vs. a locale's own week-start convention),
; and inventing an answer to a question lang_spec doesn't ask is exactly
; the kind of "design for a hypothetical requirement" this codebase
; otherwise avoids.
DURATION := DIGIT+ ("." DIGIT+)?
            ("ns"cs | "us"cs | "ms"cs | "s"cs | "m"cs | "h"cs | "d"cs)

BYTE_SIZE := DIGIT+ ("." DIGIT+)?
             ("KiB"cs | "MiB"cs | "GiB"cs | "TiB"cs | "PiB"cs
             | "KB"cs | "MB"cs | "GB"cs | "TB"cs | "PB"cs | "B"cs)

; ISO 8601 (lang_spec §4's own example: `2026-01-01T14:00:00Z`) --
; lexically permissive on purpose, not a full calendar-aware match
; (real ISO 8601 has more shapes than are worth spelling out char by
; char here: fractional seconds, a numeric UTC offset instead of `Z`,
; date-only with no time part at all). `Scry.Core.Actions` hands
; whatever this matches to Elixir's own `Date`/`NaiveDateTime`/
; `DateTime`.from_iso8601/1 -- genuine calendar validation (rejecting
; `2026-02-30`, say) happens there, confirmed empirically to surface
; correctly as `{:error, _}` through the whole pipeline, not just
; assumed. Maximal munch resolves any overlap with INTEGER/DECIMAL the
; same way RADIX already does elsewhere in this file -- a real date is
; always longer than the leading digits alone could match.
DATE := DIGIT DIGIT DIGIT DIGIT "-" DIGIT DIGIT "-" DIGIT DIGIT
        ("T" DIGIT DIGIT ":" DIGIT DIGIT ":" DIGIT DIGIT
          ("." DIGIT+)?
          ("Z" | [+-] DIGIT DIGIT ":" DIGIT DIGIT)?
        )?

; Two delimiters (lang_spec §4: "pick to avoid escaping" -- a string
; containing a `"` can be written `'...'` instead), both honoring
; ESCAPE at every position before falling back to "any character other
; than this string's own delimiter". `\"` previously had no special
; meaning at all (confirmed by actually running a query containing one
; before this existed -- it silently ended the string early, exactly
; the bug ESCAPE fixes): with no escape token tried first, `!"\"" .`
; alone treats `\` as just another ordinary character, so `\"` matched
; as "literal backslash" immediately followed by "end of string".
STRING := "\"" (ESCAPE | !"\"" .)* "\""
        | "'" (ESCAPE | !"'" .)* "'"

; lang_spec §4 lists `"""..."""` alongside the two single-quote-style
; delimiters but says nothing more about it -- no example, no stated
; escape/indentation-stripping behavior. Absent that, this is the most
; conservative reading available: content is captured verbatim between
; the delimiters (embedded newlines included, no leading-whitespace
; stripping the way e.g. Elixir's own heredocs do), honoring the same
; ESCAPE token as the two single-line forms for consistency -- mainly
; useful here for embedding a literal `"""` in content without it being
; read as the closing delimiter. Maximal munch disambiguates this from
; a pair of adjacent empty STRING tokens the same way RADIX beats
; INTEGER elsewhere in this file: `"""..."""` is strictly longer than
; whatever STRING alone could match starting from the same `"`.
MULTILINE_STRING := "\"\"\"" (ESCAPE | !"\"\"\"" .)* "\"\"\""

; \" \' \\ \n \t \uXXXX -- lang_spec §4's own escape list, exactly.
; An unrecognized `\<char>` is deliberately not rejected: ordered PEG
; choice just falls through to STRING's own "any character" fallback
; above, treating the backslash as a literal character on its own.
; Real validation of a malformed escape needs lexer-level error
; propagation this phase doesn't have.
ESCAPE := "\\" ("\"" | "'" | "\\" | "n" | "t" | "u" HEX HEX HEX HEX)

; `@tag<delim>...<delim>` (lang_spec §3/§4) is specified with "any
; delimiter" -- genuinely not expressible as a plain Aether token
; pattern for an *arbitrary*, parse-time-chosen delimiter character:
; requiring the same character back at the close is a backreference,
; and neither Aether's native token primitives nor its own regex-sigil
; desugaring support those (the latter rejects `\1`-`\9` outright, per
; guides/aether/AETHER.md's own "Regex literals" section). Handed to
; `Scry.Core.Grammar.SigilLexeme.scan/3` via Aether's own `@native`/
; `Ichor.CustomLexeme` escape hatch (real Elixir code plugged into the
; lexer) -- that module's own moduledoc has the full mechanics,
; confirmed via a scratch grammar before landing this shape (a `nil`
; capture override correctly falls through to the ordinary per-token
; path, so `Scry.Core.Actions.handle_token/3`'s own `:SIGIL` clause needs
; only a small generalization -- reading the delimiter from the matched
; text itself -- not a rewrite).
SIGIL := @native("Scry.Core.Grammar.SigilLexeme", "scan")

; ---- operators / delimiters ----

SLASH  := "/"
NOT_EQ := "not="
LE     := "<="
GE     := ">="
EQ     := "="
LT     := "<"
GT     := ">"
MATCH  := "~"
LBRACE := "{"
RBRACE := "}"
LBRACK := "["
RBRACK := "]"
COMMA  := ","
DOT    := "."
COLON  := ":"

; `TYPE`'s own union (`String | Int`) and nullable (`?Int`) markers
; (lang_spec §7) -- neither character is used anywhere else in this
; grammar, no maximal-munch/ordering conflict with any existing token.
PIPE     := "|"
QUESTION := "?"

; Arithmetic (lang_spec §5.10, §9's "Computed fields"). `POW` (`**`)
; declared before `STAR` (`*`) for readability only -- maximal munch
; already picks `POW` over two separate `STAR` matches at the same
; position regardless of declaration order, the same way `LE`/`GT`
; above don't actually depend on their own ordering either.
PLUS  := "+"
MINUS := "-"
STAR  := "*"
POW   := "**"
LPAREN := "("
RPAREN := ")"

; Fragment spread (lang_spec §5.11/§9): `...<fragment-name>` inside a
; body list. Three literal dots, strictly longer than any single `DOT`
; match at the same position -- maximal munch prefers this token the
; same way `POW` beats two separate `STAR` matches above, so no
; declaration-order sensitivity against `DOT` either (confirmed with a
; scratch grammar before relying on it, not assumed to carry over from
; the `POW`/`STAR` case just because the shape looks similar).
SPREAD := "..."

; `[]` (an empty character class) matches no character, ever -- the
; "always fails" default an extension point needs when nothing has
; contributed to it (see `select_ep1a` below). Deliberately not a
; `!`-negated lookahead on some "any character" token: that combination
; means "always *succeeds*, with zero width" (there being no character
; left to fail to find only happens at true end of input) -- the
; opposite of what's wanted here, confirmed empirically before settling
; on this instead.
NEVER := []

; ---- grammar ----

; The real root now (`@root document` above) -- a query text is zero or
; more top-level `FRAGMENT` declarations followed by exactly one `select`
; (lang_spec §6/§9: `WITH`/`TYPE` are the other two top-level
; block-openers §6 names, still deferred, not needed for `FRAGMENT` to
; work). `fragment_decl*` bare and unrenamed, same established reason
; every other repeated/optional rule reference in this file is left
; unrenamed -- confirmed empirically (scratch grammar) to behave exactly
; like `additive_tail*`/`when_clause+` here too: always produces its own
; capture key, `[]` when zero `FRAGMENT`s are present, so `select`'s own
; existing single-query callers (every test written before this feature)
; parse identically through the new root -- provably a no-op for any
; query text with no top-level `FRAGMENT`.
;
; A `FRAGMENT`'s own `...spread` references are resolved by
; `Scry.Core.FragmentResolver`, not here or in `Scry.Core.Actions` --
; that's a whole-document concern (a spread can reference a `FRAGMENT`
; declared *after* it, and a `FRAGMENT` can itself spread another one),
; not expressible as this rule walks bottom-up during an ordinary single
; pass. `document`'s own handler is what invokes it, once both
; `fragment_decl` and `select` have already been evaluated into real
; terms (`Scry.Core.Actions`' own moduledoc has the fuller story).
; Trailing `TRIVIA?` fixes the "did not expect more input here" gap
; `body_list`'s own comment flags (a real, found-not-fixed-until-now
; issue: `SELECT x { y }` followed by so much as one trailing newline
; used to fail to parse). Root cause, confirmed empirically (scratch
; grammar) before relying on it: `@skip`-splicing only ever happens
; *between* two elements of a sequence -- there's no implicit "consume
; trailing skip" step after a sequence's own last element, so trivia
; after the true final token of a top-level match was simply left in
; the token stream, and `Grammar.VM`'s own top-level match requires the
; *entire* stream consumed (`pos == tuple_size(stream)`) or it reports
; unconsumed input as a parse error. Adding one further, genuinely
; optional element to the *end* of the sequence gives the ordinary
; auto-splice inserted immediately before *it* a chance to consume that
; trailing trivia -- confirmed this is really what does the work, not
; `TRIVIA?` matching anything itself (a bare reference to the grammar's
; own `@skip` token produces no capture at all, matched or not, so this
; is invisible to `Scry.Core.Actions`' own `document` handler either
; way). Only `document` (the real root) needs this -- every other rule
; in this file is always followed by more input from its own caller,
; never genuinely at the end of the stream.
;
; The comma-optional-on-newline half of `body_list`'s own gap (lang_spec
; §6: "optional -- a line break suffices -- when each item is on its own
; line") *is* fixed here, but not the way the earlier version of this
; comment predicted. A dedicated `NEWLINE` token pulled out of skip-trivia
; turns out to be a dead end: `Grammar.VM.Tokenizer`'s own tokenizer is a
; single, whole-file, maximal-munch pass over *every* declared token,
; context-free (confirmed by reading it directly, not assumed) -- ties
; broken by declaration order, longest match wins otherwise. Any new
; token whose character class overlaps `SPACE`/`COMMA` (a `NEWLINE`
; token, or a `@native` lexeme recognizing "trivia, optional comma, more
; trivia" in one bite) inevitably ties or competes with `SPACE`/`COMMA`
; at *every* position those already appear, everywhere in the whole
; grammar, not just here -- corrupting ordinary comma-separated
; constructs (call args, `GROUP BY a, b`) and ordinary multi-line
; skip-splicing far outside `body_list`. Confirmed the hard way (a
; scratch grammar with a competing `ITEM_SEP` token parsed `{x, x}`
; fine but rejected `{x,x}` and `{x\nx}` -- ties going to whichever
; token was declared first) before abandoning that approach.
;
; `body_list_tail`'s own repetition uses two mechanisms already proven
; safe elsewhere in this file: right-recursion, and `~` (Aether's
; "suppress `@skip` splicing before this term" operator,
; `guides/aether/AETHER.md`'s own operator table -- unused anywhere else
; in this file until now). `~` only accepts a bare, unquantified,
; uncaptured token/rule reference (confirmed empirically: `~(a b)*` and
; `~name:a` both raise "~ may only prefix a bare token or rule name,
; with no capture and no quantifier" at grammar-parse time) -- which is
; exactly why `body_list_tail` below is itself a bare, right-recursive
; rule (no `*`/`+` at any `~`-suppressed reference site) rather than the
; more obvious `(sep tail:body_item)*` shape. Without `~`, the
; auto-spliced `Star(TRIVIA)` ordinary rule composition inserts between
; every sequence element would silently consume whatever trivia sits
; between two items before `body_list_sep` (below) ever got a chance to
; inspect it.
;
; That's necessary, but on its own turned out *not* to be sufficient --
; found by testing this shape against this file's own real grammar, not
; just the scratch grammar that first validated `~`+right-recursion in
; isolation. `body_item` itself (concretely: `field_body_item`'s own
; `field:path if_clause?`, and `path`'s own `(DOT tail:field_name)*`)
; has *its own* trailing, non-suppressed optional/repeated elements
; checking for an `IF`/a dotted continuation that usually isn't there.
; Ordinary `@skip` splicing before a non-first Seq element runs
; unconditionally, before the following `Opt`/`Star` even gets a chance
; to succeed or fail -- so a failed `if_clause`/dot-chain attempt still
; leaves whatever trivia it spliced past permanently gone, even though
; the attempt itself matched nothing. This is invisible to every other
; construct in this file (that trivia was headed for the skip-token
; either way) but means `body_list_sep`, entered right after `body_item`
; returns, can already be looking at a position *past* the very
; newline it needs to see -- confirmed by tracing the compiled output
; for exactly this case, not guessed. Making every such trailing
; lookahead in `body_item`'s own (large, recursive, EP1(b)/(c)/(d)-open)
; transitive grammar leak-proof one at a time isn't a one-line fix and
; isn't this round's scope.
;
; `body_list_sep` sidesteps the leak entirely instead of chasing it:
; it's rule-position `@native` (`guides/aether/AETHER.md`'s own
; `@native`/`@hint` section, "at rule position" -- distinct from the
; token-position `@native` `BLOCK_COMMENT` already uses above), handed
; the same compiled token `stream`/`pos` any ordinary rule matcher works
; over. `Scry.Core.Grammar.BodyListSep.match/4` reads `stream` directly
; -- scanning *forward* from `pos` for the ordinary "no leak happened"
; case (a `TRIVIA`/`COMMA`/`TRIVIA` run still sits right there,
; unconsumed) and *backward* from wherever that leaves it for the
; leaked case (whatever `body_item` already spliced past is still a
; real, distinct token in the stream -- just one `pos` has already moved
; beyond -- so looking behind recovers it without needing `body_item`'s
; own internals to cooperate at all). This is genuinely robust against
; the leak, unlike composing ordinary suppressed rule references, since
; it reads the stream's own ground truth rather than trusting grammar
; composition to have preserved it. Confirmed via scratch grammar
; against both `Grammar.VM` (interpreted) and the native/compiled
; backend (`use Ichor`) before landing this shape, including a
; deliberately reconstructed leak (a mock body-item shape with its own
; bare trailing `?`) to prove the forward+backward scan actually
; survives it, not just the non-leaking case the first, insufficient
; design happened to pass.
; `fragment_decl*` before `with_decl*` -- a fixed relative order between
; the two declaration kinds, not something lang_spec mandates one way or
; the other (its own §9 never shows them mixed). Simpler than a unified
; `top_level_decl := fragment_decl | with_decl` choice rule would be (no
; tag-based dispatch needed to tell a fragment declaration's own
; `{name, body_items}` result apart from a with-declaration's `{name,
; query}` one -- same shape, different meaning -- since each stays under
; its own distinct capture key instead), at the cost of requiring every
; `FRAGMENT` before every `WITH` in a document that uses both. Revisit if
; a real need to interleave them ever surfaces -- not a spec violation
; either way, just a narrower grammar than the language technically
; allows.
; `select:combined_select` -- a renamed, bare (non-quantified) rule
; reference, safe and already used throughout this file (`source:path`
; is the same shape); the "renaming produces a spurious capture"
; finding only ever applies to `?`/`*`/`+`-quantified references, never
; a plain single one like this.
;
; `type_decl*` first, before `fragment_decl*`/`with_decl*` -- lang_spec
; §7's own framing ("standalone artifacts, closer to DDL, never inline
; in a query") makes `TYPE` the most foundational, schema-level
; declaration kind, so it goes first, the same "a fixed relative order
; between declaration kinds, not something lang_spec mandates one way
; or the other" reasoning `fragment_decl*`/`with_decl*`'s own ordering
; already has (their own comment nearby). `Scry.Core.Actions`' own
; `document` handler stores every parsed `type_decl` in a `name =>
; type_decl` map on the resulting query/combined query, the same shape
; `with_bindings` already has -- but, deliberately, *only* stored, never
; read by anything else in this codebase yet (no schema-registry hook,
; no type-checking, no null-safety narrowing -- lang_spec §7's full
; type system is a real, separate, much larger undertaking than parsing
; the declaration shape). This is the exact same "grammar accepts it,
; nothing consumes it yet" posture `body_item_ep1 := NEVER`/`:variant`
; already have for an EP1(b)/(c)/(d) construct with no real kind
; contributing one yet.
;
; `*_or_comment*`, not bare `type_decl*`/etc -- `;` block comments
; (lang_spec §3, `BLOCK_COMMENT`'s own comment above) are valid
; "immediately before a block-opening keyword," which a real
; declaration and a comment *replacing* one are mutually exclusive
; alternatives at any given position (a matched `BLOCK_COMMENT` already
; consumed an entire would-be declaration's own text, so there's never
; anything left there for `type_decl`/etc itself to also try matching).
; Each wrapper is a plain, unrenamed, bare alternation (`type_decl |
; BLOCK_COMMENT`) repeated the same way `body_item` itself already is
; inside `body_list`'s own `(COMMA tail:body_item)*` -- proven safe
; already, not a new shape. `Scry.Core.Actions`' own `document` handler
; filters each resulting list down to just the real declarations
; (`is_tuple/1` -- a real one is always `{name, value}`; a matched
; `BLOCK_COMMENT`'s own default passthrough is a bare token string)
; before building the name map, so a comment contributes nothing to the
; result, exactly as if it had never been there. Verified via scratch
; grammar against `Grammar.VM` specifically (this project's own
; interpreted test-suite backend) before landing this shape -- see
; `BLOCK_COMMENT`'s own comment for why that specificity mattered here.
document := type_or_comment* fragment_or_comment* with_or_comment*
            select:combined_select TRIVIA?

type_or_comment := type_decl | BLOCK_COMMENT
fragment_or_comment := fragment_decl | BLOCK_COMMENT
with_or_comment := with_decl | BLOCK_COMMENT

; `UNION`/`UNION ALL`/`INTERSECT`/`EXCEPT` (lang_spec §5.4, "Top-level,
; joining two complete select blocks"). Bare, unrenamed
; `combinator_tail*` (renaming a repeated rule reference produces a
; spurious extra raw-text capture -- confirmed to bite here too, the
; same established finding `additive_tail*`/`fragment_decl*` already
; have, this file's own first draft of this rule got it wrong before
; catching it empirically), folded left-associative in
; `Scry.Core.Actions` (`fold_combinators/2`) -- the exact same shape
; `expression`'s own `additive_tail*` already proves safe (a dedicated
; wrapper rule per repeated element, not an inline
; `(op:(...) ...)*` group, see `additive_tail`'s own comment for why
; that distinction matters). Deliberately *not* naive right-recursion
; (`right:combined_select` on `select` itself) -- that would silently
; give `EXCEPT`/`INTERSECT` chains the *wrong* grouping (`A EXCEPT (B
; EXCEPT C)` instead of the correct `(A EXCEPT B) EXCEPT C`, since
; neither operator is commutative), a real correctness bug a naive
; shape would have baked in from the start.
;
; Combinators apply only here, at the very top of a document -- neither
; `with_decl` (still plain `select`) nor `body_item`'s own nested
; `select` reference were widened to `combined_select`. `WITH RECURSIVE`
; (lang_spec §5.4.1) is the one concrete case where a `WITH` binding
; would need to be combinable, and it's an explicitly separate, later
; increment (its own fixpoint-iteration logic, plus the graph variant's
; `VIA`, per its own worked example); revisit this boundary then, not
; before. See `Scry.Core.CombinedQuery`'s own moduledoc for the fuller
; reasoning, including why this is a real, separate result type rather
; than a field on `select`/`Query.t()` itself.
combined_select := head:select combinator_tail*

; Three top-level alternatives, disambiguated by capture-key name
; (`union`/`intersect`/`except`) -- the same established, proven-safe
; pattern `comparison`/`field_body_item` already use, not a nested
; `op:(...)` choice-group. A nested choice *would* be more compact here
; (`op:(KW_UNION KW_ALL? | KW_INTERSECT | KW_EXCEPT) right:select`), but
; every other nested choice-group in this file has needed either a
; dedicated wrapper rule (`additive_tail`'s own history) or turned out
; unsafe *inside* one depending on exactly what it contains -- a 3-way
; choice with one 2-token-sequence alternative is genuinely new,
; unverified shape here, not worth risking when three plain top-level
; alternatives are just as easy to write and already proven throughout
; this file. `all:KW_ALL?` is a bare optional *token* reference --
; always produces a capture, the literal empty string when absent,
; checked by value (`Scry.Core.Actions` tells `UNION` from `UNION ALL`
; this way, same convention `order_item`'s own `dir:(KW_DESC|KW_ASC)?`
; already uses for a different optional token).
combinator_tail := union:KW_UNION all:KW_ALL? right:select
                  | intersect:KW_INTERSECT right:select
                  | except:KW_EXCEPT right:select

; `<body-item> ...` (lang_spec §6's own template) reuses `body_list`
; directly -- a FRAGMENT's body is exactly as expressive as a SELECT's
; own (it can itself nest a SELECT, or spread another FRAGMENT), so no
; separate body grammar is needed here.
fragment_decl := KW_FRAGMENT name:field_name LBRACE body:body_list RBRACE

; `WITH <name> = SELECT ...` (lang_spec §9: "named reusable sub-query,
; SQL CTE equivalent" -- vs. `FRAGMENT`'s reusable *shape*, this is
; reusable *data*). Reuses the `select` rule directly for the bound
; value, not a copy of it -- a `WITH` binding is a real, fully-formed
; query (its own `where`/`group by`/`having`/etc. all apply exactly as
; they would standalone), lang_spec's own grammar block confirms this
; (`WITH <name> = SELECT <source> [modifiers] { <body-item> ... }`).
; `WITH RECURSIVE` (lang_spec §5.4.1) is deliberately not this rule's
; concern -- it needs `UNION`/`UNION ALL` (§5.4) to mean anything at
; all (the recursive case is defined *as* a union with a base case), and
; neither combinator exists in this grammar yet; a real, separate,
; later increment once they do.
with_decl := KW_WITH name:field_name EQ select:select

; `TYPE <name> [: <kind>] { <field>: <type> ... }` (lang_spec §7). Field
; separator is unconditionally `COMMA` -- the same pre-existing,
; already-documented "comma optional on newline" gap `body_list`'s own
; comment has (found while investigating this feature specifically: a
; `NEWLINE`-no-longer-folded-into-skip-trivia token, threaded through
; *every* separator position that should accept it, not a one-line
; fix), not a new gap this feature introduces.
type_decl := KW_TYPE name:field_name type_kind? LBRACE fields:type_field_list RBRACE
type_kind := COLON kind:IDENT

type_field_list := head:type_field (COMMA tail:type_field)*
type_field := name:field_name COLON expr:type_expr

; `<type> ::= <base-type> | ?<base-type> | <type> | <type>` (lang_spec
; §7's own EBNF, verbatim). Union binds loosest -- `type_expr` is the
; top-level entry point, `union_tail*` bare/unrenamed (the same
; established "renaming a repeated rule reference produces a spurious
; capture" finding `additive_tail*`/`combinator_tail*` already prove,
; confirmed to bite here too via scratch grammar before catching it).
; A single operand with no `union_tail` collapses to just that
; operand's own value directly (`Scry.Core.Actions`' own handler), no
; `{:union, [...]}` wrapper for the trivial one-member case -- the same
; "don't wrap what didn't need wrapping" convention `expression`'s own
; head-with-no-`additive_tail` already has.
type_expr := head:type_operand union_tail*
union_tail := PIPE operand:type_operand

; `?` is prefix (lang_spec §3: "reserves postfix position for a
; possible future conditional-access operator"), binding to the
; *immediately following* base-type only, not a trailing union chain --
; `?Int | String` means `(?Int) | String`, i.e. `Int | Nil | String`,
; not `?(Int | String)`. Bare optional *token* reference (`nullable:
; QUESTION?`), not a rule -- always produces a capture, `""` when
; absent, the same convention `order_item`'s own `dir:(KW_DESC|KW_ASC)?`
; already uses, safe to rename unlike an optional *rule* reference.
type_operand := nullable:QUESTION? base:base_type

; Three alternatives, each independently named (`shape`/`list`/`name`)
; rather than two bare passthroughs plus one named one -- the same
; "every alternative gets its own capture key" shape `in_lhs` above
; already proves safe (`Scry.Core.Actions` tells them apart by which key
; is present, not by mixing a bare-rule-reference default passthrough
; with a named one in the same rule). `name:IDENT type_param?` covers
; every bare type name (`Int`, `String`, a reference to another
; declared `TYPE`, `Json` with or without a parameter) uniformly --
; *not* gated to the literal name `"Json"` at the grammar level, the
; same "grammar stays permissive" posture `call_with_path`/`DISTINCT`
; already established for their own generic constructs (§7's own
; `Json<Type>` is the one concrete motivating case, but nothing stops
; a hypothetical future generic-shaped name from parsing the same way).
base_type := shape:shape_type | list:list_type | name:IDENT type_param?

; `type_param`/`shape_type`/`list_type` all recurse back into
; `type_expr` -- ordinary PEG recursion, not left-recursive (each
; starts with its own leading token -- `LT`/`LBRACE`/`LBRACK`
; respectively -- never `type_expr` itself at position zero), the same
; "recurses back into literal/expression, needs none of Ichor's
; left-recursion rewrite machinery" reasoning `list`/`power` already
; have elsewhere in this file.
type_param := LT inner:type_expr GT

; `Json<{ color: String, size: ?Int }>` (lang_spec §7's own worked
; example) -- an inline, anonymous shape, reusing `type_field_list`
; directly rather than requiring a separately-declared `TYPE` for every
; nested structure.
shape_type := LBRACE fields:type_field_list RBRACE

; `Json<[Type]>` (lang_spec §7) -- not gated to appearing only inside a
; `Json<...>` parameter at the grammar level, the same permissive
; posture `base_type`'s own comment already has; a general list-of-type
; shape, usable anywhere a type is expected.
list_type := LBRACK inner:type_expr RBRACK

; Modifiers appear in lang_spec.md §5.2/§6's own fixed order, mirroring
; evaluation order front to back: an EP1(a) modifier (narrows/reshapes
; the source) runs first, then where (filters), group by (groups),
; having (filters groups), distinct (dedupes the projection), order by
; (sorts), limit ... offset (paginates last). `required` is genuinely
; different from the six before it: none of them describe a step *this*
; query itself takes -- it's a flag the *enclosing* query reads, after
; this one's entire pipeline has already finished, once this select is
; used as a nested body item (lang_spec §6, "Correlation and joins").
; Last in the chain for that reason (nothing before this query's own
; closing `}` can know its final row count), not because it's "evaluated
; last" as a step of its own the way the other six are.
; Bare `goal_args?`, not `goal_args:goal_call_args?` -- found empirically
; (`Scry.Core.Actions`' own `handle_rule(:goal_args, ...)` never even
; ran under the renamed-capture form): an *explicitly renamed* optional
; capture (`name:rule?`, a different name than the rule itself) resolves
; via raw source-text concatenation of whatever the optional matched
; (the same convention `trailing_comma:COMMA?` above already relies on,
; deliberately, for a plain presence check with no structure to build) --
; not by evaluating the rule's own `handle_rule` and threading its real
; result through. A *bare* `name?` (quantified with no rename at all)
; auto-captures under its own rule name and *does* dispatch to
; `handle_rule` normally, genuinely absent (no key) when unmatched --
; exactly what a structured value like this one needs, so the rule
; itself is just named `goal_args` directly, referenced bare.
select := KW_SELECT source:path goal_args? select_ep1a?
          where_clause? group_by_clause? having_clause? distinct_clause?
          order_by_clause? limit_clause? required_clause?
          LBRACE body:body_list RBRACE

; lang_spec §8.4's `logic` variant, a call-shaped source (`SELECT
; ancestor(X, "bob") WHERE ...`). Plain core grammar, not gated to
; `logic` here -- `Scry.Core.Query`'s own moduledoc (the `goal_args`
; field) has the full "grammar exists, category check decides legality"
; reasoning. Reuses `call`'s own `call_args` production verbatim rather
; than inventing a parallel one -- an argument is any ordinary `expr()`,
; including a bare field reference, which is exactly what a logic
; variable needs to parse as (no new "this is a variable" syntax --
; `scry_logic`'s own executor decides that at execution time, per
; `Scry.Core.Query`'s own comment).
goal_args := LPAREN call_args? RPAREN

; core's EP1(a) header-modifier extension point (lang_spec §2, §5.2).
; Unlike the language spec's own text, this is *not* "exactly one kind
; may ever fill this" -- §5.2 itself has `last` (time-series) and
; `deep` (document) both nominating the same position, and a build can
; legitimately load both kinds at once. `select_ep1a` therefore always
; has a real definition (`NEVER` immediately below, when no kind
; contributes here) that `Scry.Core.GrammarCompose.merge/2` unions each
; loaded fragment's own contribution into, rather than a dangling
; reference exactly one fragment is allowed to supply. See that
; module's own moduledoc for the full reasoning and
; `Scry.Core.GrammarComposeTest` for both a one-fragment and a
; two-simultaneous-fragment proof.
select_ep1a := NEVER

; Every optional (`?`-suffixed) *rule* reference below is deliberately
; left unrenamed (`select_ep1a?`, `where_clause?`, `literal_list?` --
; never e.g. `where:where_clause?`), for the same empirically-found
; reason: explicitly naming an Opt-wrapped bare rule reference produces
; a *second*, separate raw-text capture under the explicit name
; alongside the properly dispatched one under the rule's own implicit
; self-capture name -- not a single renamed capture the way the rest of
; Aether's naming rules would suggest. Worse, the two also differ in
; whether "absent" is even detectable the same way: a *renamed* optional
; always produces a capture (raw text `""` when absent); the *bare*
; form produces no capture key at all when absent, only ever appearing
; when it actually matched. Scry.Core.Actions relies on exactly that
; distinction (`Map.has_key?/2`, not an `== ""` check) -- see its own
; moduledoc. Does not apply to optional *token* references (`KW_NOT?`
; below keeps its name fine) -- empirically, only rule references have
; this problem.

; select_ep1a := <supplied by a kind fragment>

where_clause := KW_WHERE cond:predicate

; lang_spec §5.2's three `GROUP BY` shapes -- plain, `ROLLUP(...)`,
; `CUBE(...)`. Like every other keyword here (lang_spec §3), `rollup`/
; `cube` become real reserved words once added to IDENT's own @refine
; list above -- a field genuinely named `rollup`/`cube` needs the same
; backtick escape (ESCAPED_IDENT) any other keyword-colliding field
; name would. Each alternative's own handler (Scry.Core.Actions) tags
; its result `{:plain, fields}`/`{:rollup, fields}`/`{:cube, fields}`
; -- `select`'s own handler destructures that tagged tuple into
; `Query.t()`'s own separate `group_bys`/`group_mode` fields.
group_by_clause := KW_GROUP KW_BY (group_by_rollup | group_by_cube | fields:field_list)
group_by_rollup := KW_ROLLUP LPAREN fields:field_list RPAREN
group_by_cube := KW_CUBE LPAREN fields:field_list RPAREN

having_clause := KW_HAVING cond:predicate

; A bare keyword, no argument -- `select`'s own `distinct_clause?`
; reference only ever needs to detect *presence*, not read a value out
; of this rule, so no named capture is needed here (same
; single-capture-passthrough default `comp_op`'s own bare token
; alternatives already rely on).
distinct_clause := KW_DISTINCT

order_by_clause := KW_ORDER KW_BY items:order_item_list
order_item_list := head:order_item (COMMA tail:order_item)*

; `dir:(KW_DESC | KW_ASC)?` -- a *named, optional parenthesized choice*
; of tokens -- confirmed empirically (scratch grammar, not assumed) to
; behave like every other optional *token* reference in this file
; (`neg:KW_NOT?`): absence is a real capture, the literal empty string
; `""`, checked by value -- not the `Map.has_key?/2` check a bare
; optional *rule* reference needs. It also produces a second, harmless
; spurious capture under whichever of `KW_DESC`/`KW_ASC` actually
; matched (same class of finding as `select_ep1a?`'s own history, just
; not a problem here since Scry.Core.Actions only ever reads `dir`).
;
; `key:expression`, not `field:path` -- widened from a bare field path
; to the same general `expr()` shape a `{:computed, ...}` body item's
; own value already is (lang_spec §8.5's own `ORDER BY relevance()
; DESC` needs a call here, not just a field; `ORDER BY price * quantity
; DESC`, for any kind, falls out for free from the same widening, not a
; second special case). A bare field reference through `expression` ->
; `primary` still reduces to the exact same `{:field, path}` tag every
; other `expr()` position already produces (`Scry.Core.Actions`'s own
; `primary`/`path` handler) -- the common case is unaffected in shape,
; just now wrapped, not a plain bare path list.
order_item := key:expression dir:(KW_DESC | KW_ASC)?

limit_clause  := KW_LIMIT n:INTEGER offset_clause?
offset_clause := KW_OFFSET n:INTEGER

; A bare keyword, no argument -- `select`'s own `required_clause?`
; reference only ever needs to detect *presence*, not read a value out
; of this rule, same as `distinct_clause` immediately above it in this
; file's own structure (single-capture passthrough, no named capture
; needed).
required_clause := KW_REQUIRED

field_list := head:path (COMMA tail:path)*

predicate   := disjunction
disjunction := left:conjunction (KW_OR right:conjunction)*
conjunction := left:negation (KW_AND right:negation)*
negation    := neg:KW_NOT? expr:comparison

; `in`'s own right-hand side is just `list` now, the same rule a list
; *literal* uses (`literal`'s own alternatives below) -- `in [...]`
; never needed its own bracket-handling, `list` already covers a
; possibly-empty, comma-separated `literal` sequence in brackets.
;
; `right_field:path`, a second alternative distinguished from
; `right:literal` by capture-key name alone -- the same proven pattern
; that already tells this rule's own `in` alternative apart from the
; literal one, not something new. Lexically unambiguous: every one of
; `literal`'s own alternatives starts with a quote/digit/`[`/`:`/`@`/a
; refined `KW_*` token, never a plain `IDENT`/`ESCAPED_IDENT` the way
; `path` always does, so there's no PEG ambiguity between the two
; `right*` alternatives, no ordering sensitivity either (lang_spec §5.9:
; "Every comparison's right-hand side may be another field path, not
; only a literal" -- `WHERE price > cost` compares two fields of the
; same row; this is also the mechanism a nested SELECT uses to
; correlate to an enclosing row, Scry.Core.Executor's own moduledoc).
; `left:predicate_lhs`, not `left:path` -- lang_spec §5.8's own worked
; `HAVING sum(total) > 200` needs a function call on a comparison's
; *left*-hand side, which `path` alone can never be. `predicate_lhs`
; (below) is `call_with_path | call | path`, so a bare field comparison
; still resolves to exactly the same plain `[String.t()]` as before
; (default single-capture passthrough, zero renamed captures involved --
; every existing predicate is unaffected). Deliberately narrower than
; `right`'s own `literal | right_field:path` widening this session
; already did -- the right-hand side is *not* widened to accept a call
; too (`HAVING sum(a) > avg(b)`, a call on *both* sides, stays
; unsupported); the one concrete need (lang_spec §11's own worked
; example) is call-on-the-left-only. `call_with_path` (lang_spec §5.8's
; own `json(<field>)` worked example, `WHERE json(metadata).color =
; "red"`) is what makes a call's own *result* reachable via an ordinary
; dot-path afterward -- see `call_with_path`'s own comment near `call`
; below for the fuller reasoning.
; `in`'s own right-hand side also accepts a *computed* list -- a plain
; field path, a bare call, or a call narrowed by a dot-path -- not just
; a bracketed list literal. Found while testing `json(<field>).path`
; (this file's own `call_with_path` comment): lang_spec §7's own worked
; example, `WHERE "urgent" in metadata.tags`, plus the analogous
; `WHERE "urgent" in json(metadata).tags` for a JSON-typed field,
; neither parsed at all before this -- `items:list` only ever matched a
; literal `[...]`. Three new alternatives under a *different* capture
; key (`items_expr`, not `items`) rather than widening `list` itself:
; `list` stays exactly what a list *literal* is (still needed for
; `x in [1, 2, 3]`), and `items_expr`'s own three sub-alternatives are
; ordered `call_with_path | call | path`, the same proven-safe order
; `predicate_lhs` (below) already uses for the exact same three shapes,
; confirmed again here with a scratch grammar before committing to it
; (a plain path like `metadata` and a call like `json(metadata)` are
; only lexically distinguishable by whether `(` follows, so trying the
; more specific shapes first and letting backtracking fall through is
; required, not incidental). `Scry.Core.Actions`' own handler tells the
; four `in` alternatives apart by capture-key name alone (`items` vs
; `items_expr`), the same "different alternatives, different capture
; keys" convention `right`/`right_field` above already established --
; confirmed the parser dispatches to the right one for all four shapes
; via the same scratch grammar. `Scry.Core.Executor`'s own `{:in, lhs,
; values}` predicate tag is what actually resolves either shape at
; execution time (a real Elixir list of elements vs a single expr()
; expected to resolve to the whole list) -- see its own moduledoc.
;
; `left:in_lhs`, not `left:predicate_lhs`, on all four `in` alternatives
; specifically -- lang_spec §7's own worked example (quoted verbatim
; above) has a *literal* on `in`'s own left, `"urgent" in metadata.tags`,
; not a field/call; `predicate_lhs` alone can never produce that.
; `in_lhs` (below) is `predicate_lhs`'s own three shapes plus a fourth,
; `literal` -- deliberately scoped to `in` alone, not applied to `=`/`>`/
; etc.'s own `left:predicate_lhs` above, since no worked example needs a
; literal on *those* operators' left side (`"x" = status` has no real
; use lang_spec calls for; `HAVING sum(total) > 200` is the one that
; matters there, already covered). `literal`'s own `list` alternative
; (`[1, 2] in ...`) is real overlap risk here: both a plain field path
; and a literal list literal are, once resolved, an ordinary Elixir
; list -- `Scry.Core.Executor.resolve_predicate_lhs/4`'s existing
; catch-all clause already treats *any* plain list it receives as field-
; path segments to walk into the row, so a raw `[1, 2]` literal handed
; to it unwrapped would be silently misinterpreted as a two-segment
; field path, not a value -- wrong, not just uncaught. `in_lhs`'s own
; `lhs_literal:literal` alternative is wrapped `{:literal, value}` by
; `Scry.Core.Actions` specifically to prevent that collision (a 2-tuple
; never matches the existing bare-list/`{:call, ...}`/`{:dot, ...}`
; clauses), confirmed via the same scratch grammar as the `items_expr`
; finding above -- `predicate_lhs`'s own three shapes stay completely
; unwrapped, unaffected, exactly as before.
comparison := left:predicate_lhs op:comp_op right:literal
            | left:predicate_lhs op:comp_op right_field:path
            | left:in_lhs in:KW_IN items:list
            | left:in_lhs in:KW_IN items_expr:call_with_path
            | left:in_lhs in:KW_IN items_expr:call
            | left:in_lhs in:KW_IN items_expr:path
            | comparison_ep1e

; core's EP1(e) infix comparison-tier operator extension point (lang_
; spec §2, §8.5's `SEARCH`) -- the third extension point, after
; `select_ep1a`/`body_item_ep1` above. Unlike either of those, exactly
; one kind may ever fill this in practice today (only `search` nominates
; this position at all), but it's still built the same union-of-
; simultaneous-contributions way (`NEVER` default, `Scry.Core.
; GrammarCompose.merge/2` unions each loaded fragment's own
; contribution via `union_rule/2`) rather than a dangling reference
; exactly one fragment is allowed to supply -- consistent with the
; other two, not a special case, and future-proof if a second kind ever
; nominates an infix operator here too.
comparison_ep1e := NEVER

; comparison_ep1e := <supplied by a kind fragment, e.g.
;   left:predicate_lhs KW_SEARCH right:STRING>
;
; A kind's own fragment produces a `predicate()` core cannot interpret
; on its own -- tagged `{:variant, term()}` by that kind's own Actions,
; the exact same opaque-to-core idiom `body_item_ep1`'s own `{:variant,
; term()}` body-item shape already established (`Scry.Core.Query`'s own
; moduledoc has the full reasoning for that one; this is its predicate-
; tree counterpart). A kind package contributing here must fully lower
; every `{:variant, ...}` predicate leaf, anywhere in `wheres`/`havings`
; (including nested inside `and`/`or`/`not`), before ever calling `Scry.
; Core.Executor.run/3,4` -- the same "what a kind package must
; guarantee" contract `Scry.Core.EngineBehaviour`'s own moduledoc
; already states for `query.variant` and a `:variant` body item, now
; extended to cover this new leaf shape too.

predicate_lhs := call_with_path | call | path

in_lhs := lhs_expr:call_with_path | lhs_expr:call | lhs_expr:path | lhs_literal:literal

comp_op := LE | GE | NOT_EQ | EQ | LT | GT | MATCH

literal_list := head:literal (COMMA tail:literal)*

list := LBRACK items:literal_list? RBRACK

; `rational` before bare INTEGER: both start with the same INTEGER
; token, so ordered-PEG-choice would otherwise never get a chance to try
; the SLASH-containing alternative -- ordinary backtracking makes this
; safe (see priv/grammar.aether's own `body_item` comment for the same
; convention elsewhere). Deliberately plain-INTEGER-only on both sides
; (not DECIMAL) -- lang_spec.md §4's own example is `2/3`, not a
; division of two arbitrary exact rationals, which would need real
; division, not just `Scry.Core.Rational.new/2`'s construct-and-reduce.
;
; `list` recurses back into `literal` (a list of lists, `[[1,2],[3]]`,
; is valid) -- ordinary PEG recursion, not left-recursive (`list`
; starts with the LBRACK token, never `literal` itself at position
; zero), so it needs none of Ichor's left-recursion rewrite machinery,
; same as `select`/`body_item`'s own recursion elsewhere in this file.
literal := STRING | MULTILINE_STRING | DATE | rational | DECIMAL | RADIX | DURATION | BYTE_SIZE
         | INTEGER | list | ATOM | SIGIL | PARAM | KW_NIL | KW_TRUE | KW_FALSE

rational := numerator:INTEGER SLASH denominator:INTEGER

path := head:field_name (DOT tail:field_name)*

field_name := IDENT | ESCAPED_IDENT

; Arithmetic expressions (lang_spec §5.10, §9's "Computed fields") --
; standard precedence climbing, low to high: additive (`+ -`) <
; multiplicative (`* /`) < power (`**`, right-associative) < primary.
; No function calls yet (`sum(...)` etc., §5.8) -- those are aggregate
; functions tied to `group by`/`having`, neither executed anywhere in
; this codebase yet either, so there's nothing real to call them
; against; a separate, later increment.
;
; Each binary level uses a *dedicated* tail rule (`additive_tail`,
; `mult_tail`), not an inline `(op:(...) right:...)*` group -- found
; empirically to matter, not a style preference: naming a choice group
; (`op:(PLUS | MINUS)`) directly inside an inline *repeated* group
; scrambles the captures (the per-repetition `op`/`right` correlation
; is lost entirely; `PLUS`/`MINUS` end up as separate, flat,
; disconnected lists at the outer rule's own level instead). Wrapping
; each repeated element in its own named rule sidesteps this
; completely -- confirmed with a scratch grammar before committing to
; either shape, the same discipline every other capture-shape finding
; in this file already followed. `additive_tail*`/`mult_tail*` are
; left unrenamed for the usual reason (`select_ep1a?`'s own comment):
; renaming a repeated rule reference produces the same kind of spurious
; extra capture a renamed *optional* one does, confirmed to extend to
; `*` here too, not just `?`.
expression := left:multiplicative additive_tail*
additive_tail := op:(PLUS | MINUS) right:multiplicative

multiplicative := left:power mult_tail*
mult_tail := op:(STAR | SLASH) right:power

; Right-associative by construction: `exp:power` recurses into another
; `power`, not `primary`, so `2 ** 3 ** 2` parses as `2 ** (3 ** 2)`,
; matching every other language's own `**`/`^` convention -- verified
; against a scratch grammar, not assumed from the shape alone.
power := base:primary (POW exp:power)?

; `call_with_path` before `call` before `path` -- all three start with
; `IDENT`, so ordered-choice backtracking (the same convention
; `alias:field_name COLON` before the plain-field alternative already
; uses in `field_body_item`) is what makes trying the more specific
; shapes first safe: no `LPAREN` immediately after the name fails
; `call_with_path`/`call`'s own alternatives and falls through, in
; order, consuming nothing extra; a `call` with no following `DOT`
; falls through from `call_with_path` to plain `call` the exact same
; way. `sum`/`count`/etc. (lang_spec §5.8) are deliberately *not*
; reclassified keywords -- a bare `sum` with no parens must keep parsing
; as an ordinary field path (e.g. a real field literally named "sum"),
; only `IDENT LPAREN ... RPAREN` triggers `call`/`call_with_path` at
; all.
; `window_call` tried before `call_with_path`/`call` -- the same "more
; specific first, backtracking falls through cleanly" pattern
; `call_with_path | call` already proves (below): a call with a
; trailing `OVER` matches `window_call` fully; one without it fails
; `window_call` outright (no partial match to backtrack out of -- `KW_OVER`
; is required immediately after `call`) and falls through to
; `call_with_path`/`call` exactly as before, confirmed empirically via
; scratch grammar together with every other shape in this comment
; block. `window_call` and `call_with_path` are mutually exclusive on
; any given input (a window function's own `OVER` attaches directly to
; the call, never combined with a trailing `.path` -- lang_spec §5.5's
; own grammar row shows no such combination), so their relative order
; doesn't matter for correctness, only readability.
primary := literal | window_call | call_with_path | call | path | when_expr
         | LPAREN inner:expression RPAREN

; lang_spec §5.8's fixed built-in-function surface -- "anything else
; called as `identifier(args)` is either an EP2 namespaced extension
; call, or (against a `logic`-kind source) `logic`'s EP2 bare call" --
; so this rule accepts *any* identifier followed by parenthesized args,
; not just the 15 real functions this phase executes (`sum`/`avg`/
; `count`/`min`/`max`/`stddev_samp`/`stddev_pop`/`var_samp`/`var_pop`/
; `percentile`, `string`/`int`/`exact`/`inexact`/`json`, plus the 4
; window-only names below). Which names are actually executable is
; `Scry.Core.Executor`'s own concern (`eval_aggregate/5`/`apply_cast/2`),
; not the grammar's -- same split as `body_item_ep1`/`:variant`
; elsewhere in this file.
;
; `call_args` is *optional* now (bare, unrenamed -- not `args:
; call_args?`) -- lang_spec §5.8's `row_number()`/`rank()` (§5.5,
; window functions) are genuinely zero-argument, the first real need
; for this. Bare, not renamed, for the same reason documented at
; length on `list`'s own `items:literal_list?` above: renaming an
; *optional* rule reference produces a spurious second, raw-text
; capture under the new name, while the real dispatch still only ever
; happens under the rule's own implicit name (`call_args`, not `args`)
; -- confirmed (again) via scratch grammar before committing to this
; shape. `Scry.Core.Actions`' own `call` handler reads it the same way
; `list`'s own handler already reads `:literal_list` (`Map.has_key?/2`,
; absent -> `[]`), not a value it happens to call `args`.
call := name:IDENT LPAREN call_args? RPAREN

; `json(<field>)` (lang_spec §5.8/§7: "reinterprets a String field for
; one qualified use -- `WHERE json(metadata).color = "red"`") is the one
; real motivating case for this -- a call's own *result* narrowed by an
; ordinary dot-path afterward. Not gated to the name "json" specifically
; at the grammar level, the same "grammar stays permissive, execution
; rejects misuse" posture `DISTINCT` (`call_arg`, above) already has --
; `Scry.Core.Executor`'s own `{:dot, ...}` resolution is what actually
; requires the base value be map-shaped, whatever produced it. Reuses
; `path` directly for everything after the first `DOT` (confirmed
; empirically, scratch grammar, that ordered-choice backtracking falls
; through to plain `call` cleanly when there's no trailing dot-path, and
; to plain `path` when there's no call at all).
call_with_path := call:call DOT path:path

; `count(distinct …)` (lang_spec §5.8: "Distinct-value count") -- `DISTINCT`
; is allowed syntactically as a prefix on *any* call argument here, not
; just `count`'s own single one, the same "grammar stays permissive,
; execution rejects misuse with a clear error" posture `call` itself
; already has for an unknown function name (`Scry.Core.Executor.
; eval_aggregate/5`'s own comment). Reuses `KW_DISTINCT` directly -- the
; exact same token `distinct_clause` (`select`'s own header modifier)
; already reclassifies from `IDENT`, no new keyword needed. The
; non-`DISTINCT` alternative passes straight through to whatever
; `expression` itself produces (confirmed empirically, scratch grammar
; -- `call_arg`'s own second alternative needs no `handle_rule` clause
; at all, the same default single-capture passthrough `field_name :=
; IDENT | ESCAPED_IDENT` already relies on), so every existing call
; (none of which use `DISTINCT`) is completely unaffected.
call_args := head:call_arg (COMMA tail:call_arg)*
call_arg := distinct:KW_DISTINCT expr:expression | expression

; Window functions (lang_spec §5.5: "`<fn>() OVER [PARTITION BY
; <field>,...] [ORDER BY <field> [desc|asc],...] [ROWS BETWEEN <bound>
; AND <bound>]`"). `OVER` marks a call as a window function -- reachable
; from `primary` (above), so it's syntactically permitted on *any*
; call, the same "grammar stays permissive, execution rejects misuse"
; posture `DISTINCT`/`call_with_path` already have (a non-window
; function used with `OVER`, or a window-only function like
; `row_number` used *without* it, both raise a clear error at execution
; time -- `Scry.Core.Executor`'s own concern). No parens around
; `over_spec` itself -- lang_spec's own worked examples never show any
; (`row_number() OVER PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary DESC`),
; unlike SQL's `OVER (...)`.
window_call := call:call KW_OVER over:over_spec

; All three pieces optional and in this fixed order (lang_spec's own
; `[PARTITION BY ...] [ORDER BY ...] [ROWS BETWEEN ...]`), the same
; "fixed modifier order, each independently optional" convention
; `select`'s own header-modifier chain above already established.
; Bare/unrenamed for the usual reason (`list`'s own `items:
; literal_list?` comment) -- confirmed via scratch grammar, together
; with `call_args?` above, before committing to this shape.
over_spec := partition_clause? order_by_clause? frame_clause?

; Reuses `field_list` verbatim -- `group_by_clause`'s own field-list
; rule above, already exactly "comma-separated `path`s," no new rule
; needed.
partition_clause := KW_PARTITION KW_BY fields:field_list

; Reuses `order_by_clause` verbatim (above, `select`'s own top-level
; `ORDER BY`) for a window's own *internal* ordering -- identical shape
; (`path (KW_DESC|KW_ASC)?`, comma-separated), no duplicate rule
; needed; a window's own ORDER BY and a query's top-level one are
; syntactically and semantically the same construct, just used in two
; different positions.
;
; lang_spec §5.5: "omitting `rows between` always means the whole
; partition, regardless of whether `order by` is present... Ranking
; functions (`row_number()`, `rank()`) ignore frame entirely." Reuses
; the existing `KW_AND`/`KW_BY` tokens directly -- no new keyword
; needed for either.
frame_clause := KW_ROWS KW_BETWEEN start:frame_bound KW_AND stop:frame_bound

; lang_spec §5.5: "bound ∈ `UNBOUNDED PRECEDING`, `<n> PRECEDING`,
; `CURRENT ROW`, `<n> FOLLOWING`, `UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING`". `KW_UNBOUNDED`/
; `n:INTEGER` share one alternative each for PRECEDING/FOLLOWING
; (`prec_or_foll:(KW_PRECEDING | KW_FOLLOWING)`, a named *token* choice
; group -- proven safe by `order_item`'s own `dir:(KW_DESC | KW_ASC)?`
; immediately above, the same shape) rather than 4 separate
; alternatives, since `Scry.Core.Actions` needs to know which of the two
; matched either way and a token choice group doesn't have the
; capture-scrambling problem a *rule* choice group nested in a
; *repeated* group has (this file's own `additive_tail` comment) --
; neither condition applies here (not repeated, not a rule choice).
frame_bound := KW_UNBOUNDED prec_or_foll:(KW_PRECEDING | KW_FOLLOWING)
             | n:INTEGER prec_or_foll:(KW_PRECEDING | KW_FOLLOWING)
             | KW_CURRENT KW_ROW

; Conditional expression (lang_spec §5.6, §9): "Inline, not a block."
; Just another `primary` alternative, not a separate top-level-only
; construct -- lang_spec §9's own framing ("any body item may be an
; arbitrary expression") makes a WHEN/THEN/ELSE conceptually the same
; kind of thing as any other expression, so it composes the same way
; (nests inside arithmetic, parentheses, another WHEN's own branches).
; `when_clause+` (one-or-more, not `*`) puts "at least one WHEN...THEN"
; directly in the grammar itself, matching lang_spec §5.6's own text,
; rather than a separate check later. `ELSE` is mandatory the same way
; -- no `?`, no default. Bare (unrenamed) `when_clause+`, the same
; established reason every other repeated rule reference in this file
; is left unrenamed (confirmed to extend to `+` too, not just `*`/`?`,
; the same way it already does for `*` -- verified with a scratch
; grammar before relying on it here, not assumed to carry over).
when_expr := when_clause+ KW_ELSE else_expr:expression

; `cond:predicate` reaches forward to `predicate`'s own definition
; below (Aether allows this; a definition may reference a name declared
; later in the file) rather than duplicating boolean-logic grammar --
; the exact same `predicate` a `WHERE` clause already uses, and
; therefore `WHEN`'s own condition can already do anything `WHERE` can
; (comparisons, `and`/`or`/`not`, `in [...]`, `~`, ...), for free.
when_clause := KW_WHEN cond:predicate KW_THEN then_expr:expression

; A body item is a nested SELECT (core-only, no extension needed --
; ordinary PEG recursion, same rule referencing itself indirectly
; through body_list), a kind-contributed construct (EP1(b)/(c)/(d)
; body-item shapes -- a block-opening construct, a scoped pseudo-field,
; or a bare value pseudo-field; lang_spec §2's impl_spec companion), a
; fragment spread (lang_spec §5.11/§9: `...<fragment-name>`), or a plain
; field path, optionally suffixed `IF $<param>` (lang_spec §5.3/§9:
; conditional field inclusion, GraphQL `@include`/`@skip` equivalent) --
; tried in that order (most specific leading token first, generic
; fallback last; PEG's first-match-wins doesn't actually need the
; ordering here, since all four start with structurally distinct
; tokens, but it's the same defensive convention select_ep1a's own
; alternatives follow).
body_item := select | body_item_ep1 | spread | field_body_item

; `{:spread, name}` (`Scry.Core.Actions`' own handle_rule) is a
; placeholder, not a real body-item shape -- `Scry.Core.FragmentResolver`
; replaces every one with the named FRAGMENT's own (already-resolved)
; body-item list before `Scry.Core.parse/1` ever returns, so it never
; reaches `Query.body_item()`'s own public type. See `document`'s own
; comment above for why resolution can't happen locally, right here,
; the way every other body-item shape resolves itself.
spread := SPREAD name:field_name

; `alias:field_name COLON ...` before the plain-field alternative --
; both start with the same field_name/path-leading tokens, so ordered
; backtracking (same convention as `rational` before bare `INTEGER`)
; is what makes trying the more specific shape first safe: no `COLON`
; immediately after the name fails this alternative and falls through
; to the plain field, consuming nothing extra.
;
; A space (or any non-identifier character) is required right after
; the colon for this to parse as intended -- confirmed empirically,
; not a style nicety: `subtotal:price` (no space) tokenizes as
; `IDENT("subtotal")` + `ATOM(":price")` instead, since maximal munch
; always prefers ATOM's longer match over a bare COLON whenever `:` is
; immediately followed by an identifier character. `subtotal: price`
; (the worked example's own spacing, lang_spec §9) parses correctly.
; Not something fixable at the grammar level -- it's the lexer having
; already committed to the wrong token before the parser ever runs.
field_body_item := alias:field_name COLON expr:expression
                  | field:path if_clause?

; Always `$<param>` specifically (lang_spec §5.3: "`<field> IF
; $<param>`"), not a general expression -- lang_spec's own body-item
; grammar (§6) lists `IF` only on a plain `<field>`, never on
; `<alias>: <expression>`, even now that a general expression grammar
; exists.
if_clause := KW_IF param:PARAM

; core's EP1(b)/(c)/(d) body-item extension point -- the same
; union-of-simultaneous-contributions design as select_ep1a (this
; file's own comment there has the full reasoning); a real kind
; contributes its own block-opening construct, pseudo-field, or value
; pseudo-field here (VIA, PARENT, SIBLINGS, ANCESTORS, PATH, ... in the
; language spec's own vocabulary), and more than one kind can
; contribute simultaneously.
body_item_ep1 := NEVER

; A real, found-not-yet-fixed gap against lang_spec §6's own comma rule
; ("required when two items share a physical line, optional -- a line
; break suffices -- when each item is on its own line"): `COMMA` is
; unconditionally required here, on every line break or not -- this
; Phase 1 grammar has no "or a line break" alternative yet, so e.g.
; `{ name\n email }` (no comma) fails to parse, only `{ name,\n email }`
; or `{ name, email }` do. Found while writing the FRAGMENT feature's
; own multi-line test fixtures (not something FRAGMENT introduces --
; `select` alone had the identical gap); fixing it for real needs a
; dedicated `NEWLINE` token no longer folded into skip-trivia (`document`
; 's own comment nearby has the fuller reasoning for why this is a
; bigger, separate increment, not a one-line fix). The sibling gap this
; comment used to also describe -- trailing trivia after a top-level
; query's own final `RBRACE` not being consumed -- is fixed now, by
; `document`'s own trailing `TRIVIA?`.
; Trailing `COMMA?` -- lang_spec §6's own "trailing comma permitted",
; deliberately handled with plain default splicing (unlike the
; mid-list separator below) since there's no same-line-vs-newline
; distinction to make once the list is already ending: a trailing comma
; is always optional, full stop, whether or not a newline follows it.
body_list := head:body_item ~body_list_tail trailing_comma:COMMA?

; Right-recursive on purpose (`document`'s own nearby comment has the
; full reasoning for why this shape, not a `(body_list_sep
; tail:body_item)*` quantified group, is what makes `~`-suppression
; possible at all here). `~body_list_sep` and the recursive `~
; body_list_tail` are both bare, unquantified, uncaptured references
; (auto-captured under their own rule name regardless -- Aether's own
; "a bare reference to a named token or rule is implicitly captured
; under its own name" rule, unaffected by `~`), satisfying `~`'s own
; "bare reference only" restriction while still handing
; `Scry.Core.Actions` a real value to walk. `Scry.Core.Actions`' own
; `body_list_tail` handler is what turns this right-recursive chain
; back into a flat list, and what forces `body_list_sep`'s own
; same-line validation to actually run (`body_list_sep`'s capture is a
; bare `:ok`, otherwise unused, evaluated purely for that
; side effect -- documented on the handler itself).
body_list_tail := (~body_list_sep tail:body_item ~body_list_tail)?

; Comma required when two items share a physical line, optional (a line
; break suffices) when each is on its own line, lang_spec §6's own
; wording -- validated in `Scry.Core.Actions`' own `body_list_sep`
; handler, not expressible as a hard grammar-level rejection (a
; same-line, no-comma gap has to parse structurally, the same as every
; other shape here, so the error can carry a clear message instead of
; the generic, positionless "input does not match" a raw grammar
; failure would give). Always succeeds, zero-width or not (`@hint
; (nullable: true)`) -- `Scry.Core.Grammar.BodyListSep.match/4`'s own
; moduledoc has the full scan design; the *only* thing it hands back is
; a `status` of `"ok"` or `"missing_comma"`, resolved into `:ok` or a
; real `{:error, ...}` by `Scry.Core.Actions`' own handler, the same
; "grammar overmatches, an Actions callback decides validity" split
; `BLOCK_COMMENT` and the JS string-interpolation example in Ichor's own
; guides both already use.
body_list_sep := @native("Scry.Core.Grammar.BodyListSep", "match") @hint(nullable: true)
